# ============================ ACCOUNTS 1 ============================
LESSONS_A1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Books that write themselves", "11", "<p>This track certifies you on the accounts layer of ZhiftERP: the General Ledger, the Chart of Accounts, journals, banking, receivables and payables, tax, dimensions, the period close, and the settings that govern all of it. This module covers the foundation: how the ledger works, how the chart is designed, and how to read both.</p><p><b>The General Ledger.</b> Every financial event in the business becomes a dated entry in the General Ledger (GL): an account, a debit or credit, an amount, and the <b>document that caused it</b>. Entries are added and never edited — corrections are new entries posted beside the old ones (module 2 covers the rules). The GL is the money twin of the stock ledger: both are permanent records written by documents, and some documents write both at once — a delivery moves stock <b>and</b> posts the cost of goods sold in the same act.</p><p><b>The central idea: the books are written by documents.</b> In ZhiftERP, a properly used trading system produces its own books:</p><p>• A sales invoice posts the revenue, the VAT, and the customer's debt.<br>• A purchase receipt posts the stock value and the accrual for the unbilled goods.<br>• A payment entry posts the bank movement and clears the debt.</p><p>Nobody journalises any of this — each document carries its accounting inside it. The accounting work therefore changes shape: instead of writing entries all day, you govern the machine — the chart it posts into (this module), the few manual entries it genuinely needs (module 2), the reconciliations that prove it (module 3), and the statements it produces (module 7). A business that hand-journalises its sales while the trading modules sit idle has declined the system's whole premise; recognising that situation, and knowing how to fix it, is part of this certification.</p><p><b>What this module teaches.</b> Chapter 2: debits and credits — the two-word grammar every GL entry speaks. Chapters 3-4: the Chart of Accounts — its tree structure, its design rules, and the account types that wire accounts into the machinery. Chapter 5: the posting map — exactly which entries each document writes; the reference card for the whole track. Chapter 6: reading the GL and the trial balance. Chapter 7: control accounts and party ledgers — the tie between a total and its details. Chapter 8: the case study — <b>HealthTrade</b>, the pharmaceutical distributor this track follows, designing its chart in one afternoon. Chapter 9: review and the module's law.</p><p><b>One orientation before starting.</b> The chart and the ledger are read far more often than they are written. Every design decision in this module is judged by one question: does it make the books easier to read?</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Debits and credits", "12", "<p>Every GL entry uses two words — debit and credit. Learn the grammar once in this chapter and every entry in the system becomes readable.</p><p><b>The equation.</b> The books describe one reality twice: <b>Assets = Liabilities + Equity</b> — what the business has equals who has claims on it — with income and expenses as equity's running story. Every event touches at least two accounts, and every entry's debits equal its credits: money that came from somewhere went somewhere, and the entry records both ends.</p><p><b>The direction table.</b> Memorise this; it is the whole grammar:</p><p>• <b>Assets</b> (bank, debtors, stock, equipment) — <b>debit to increase</b>, credit to decrease.<br>• <b>Expenses</b> (rent, salaries, COGS) — <b>debit to increase</b>. Expenses move like assets, which is the table's one merciful simplification.<br>• <b>Liabilities</b> (creditors, VAT payable, loans) — <b>credit to increase</b>, debit to decrease.<br>• <b>Income</b> (sales, other income) — <b>credit to increase</b>.<br>• <b>Equity</b> (capital, retained earnings) — <b>credit to increase</b>.</p><p>The mnemonic: <b>DEAD</b> — <b>D</b>ebits increase <b>E</b>xpenses, <b>A</b>ssets, <b>D</b>rawings; everything else grows by credit.</p><p><b>Three familiar documents, read in the grammar.</b></p><p>1. <b>A sales invoice for ₦107,500</b> (₦100,000 + VAT): DEBIT Debtors ₦107,500 (an asset grew — the customer owes you), CREDIT Sales ₦100,000 (income grew), CREDIT VAT Payable ₦7,500 (a liability grew — tax money held in custody for FIRS). Debits ₦107,500 = credits ₦107,500.<br>2. <b>The customer's payment:</b> DEBIT Bank ₦107,500 (asset up), CREDIT Debtors ₦107,500 (asset down — the claim became cash).<br>3. <b>A purchase receipt of stock at ₦940,000:</b> DEBIT Stock ₦940,000 (asset up), CREDIT Stock Received But Not Billed ₦940,000 (a liability — you hold goods you have not yet been invoiced for; Chapter 5 completes this thread).</p><p><b>What the grammar lets you read.</b></p><p>• <b>The balance side.</b> Each account normally carries one side. Debtors carries a debit balance; a <b>credit</b> balance on a customer means you owe <b>them</b> — usually an unallocated advance. The wrong-side balance is always a story worth opening.<br>• <b>The growth read.</b> Credits growing Sales is trade; debits growing Sales is returns and reversals — worth a look when large.<br>• <b>The other side.</b> Every entry has a counterparty account. When an entry looks wrong, read its other side — the expense that was credited, the bank that was debited twice — and the mistake usually names itself.</p><p>Practical rule: when any balance or entry confuses you, say it out loud in the grammar — which account, which side, grew by what. Most confusion does not survive the sentence.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Chart of Accounts: designing the tree", "12", "<p>Every account lives in the <b>Chart of Accounts</b>, which is a tree: <b>group</b> nodes for organising and reading, and <b>ledger accounts</b> — the leaves — for posting. Every GL entry names a leaf; posting to a group fails by design. Money exists in accounts; groups exist so accounts can be read together.</p><p><b>The fixed roots.</b> Four (or five) root types anchor everything: <b>Assets, Liabilities, Equity, Income, Expenses</b> — the equation's own families. Beneath them, groups organise for reading (Current Assets → Bank Accounts → the individual banks; Indirect Expenses → Administrative → the specific lines), and the leaves take the postings.</p><p><b>Design from the reading, not the listing.</b> The standard chart the system ships is a competent skeleton. The design work is tailoring it to how <b>this business</b> asks questions:</p><p>1. Write down the owner's monthly questions first — what did we make, what did it cost, where did the money go. These define the P&amp;L groups.<br>2. Add the statement's needs and the audit's. These define the balance-sheet shape.<br>3. Apply the granularity test to every proposed account: <b>an account earns its existence when someone will read its line separately.</b> Three bank accounts are three leaves because three reconciliations will happen. Office Expenses split into eleven micro-accounts nobody reads separately is sprawl.</p><p><b>Accounts versus dimensions.</b> A distinction nobody reads as its own account line belongs to a <b>dimension</b>, not an account. The chart answers <b>what kind</b> of money; dimensions (module 6) answer <b>whose and where</b> — the branch, the service line. Mixing them up is how charts explode into per-branch copies of every expense line. Rule: one chart, one language; the branches become a lens on it, not copies of it.</p><p><b>Naming, numbering, and depth.</b></p><p>• <b>Names</b> that read plainly in every report — the accountant's vocabulary, not the software's.<br>• <b>Numbering</b> where the business's reporting tradition uses it: consistent, gapped for insertion, never re-used.<br>• <b>Shape:</b> shallow. Two to three levels below root serves almost every trading business; a five-level chart is bureaucracy nobody navigates.</p><p><b>Change discipline.</b> Charts live for decades:</p><p>• <b>Renaming</b> a leaf is cheap — the history follows the account.<br>• <b>Restructuring</b> — re-parenting groups, merging accounts — changes how history reads, so it is planned, done at a period boundary, and documented.<br>• <b>Creation rights are narrow.</b> New accounts are proposed against the reading test, not created per whim. Chapter 9's sprawl pattern is what happens otherwise.</p><p>Practical rule: the finished chart should pass a one-line test — every account has a one-sentence explanation of who reads it and why. An account that fails the test should not exist.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Account types: the wiring", "12", "<p>Two leaves can sit side by side in the tree and behave completely differently, because beneath the position sits the <b>account type</b> — and the type is what wires an account into the system's machinery. Names are for humans; types are for the machine.</p><p><b>The types and what each enables.</b></p><p>• <b>Bank</b> and <b>Cash</b> — join the payment machinery: payment modes point at them, and — decisively — Bank-type accounts join the <b>bank reconciliation</b> (module 3). A real-world bank account modelled as any other type is invisible to the reconciliation that proves it.<br>• <b>Receivable</b> and <b>Payable</b> — the control accounts: entries here <b>require a party</b> (the customer or supplier). This is what makes per-party ledgers possible — the control account is the total, the party is the detail (Chapter 7). An invoice posted to a non-Receivable account has left the machinery that ages, allocates and produces statements.<br>• <b>Stock</b> — the accounts the perpetual-inventory machinery writes: stock-in-hand, the received-not-billed accrual, the adjustment homes. Manual journals against Stock-type accounts are fenced, because the stock ledger and the GL must stay in agreement (module 2, Chapter 6).<br>• <b>Tax</b> — the VAT and WHT accounts the tax templates post to: typed so the tax machinery and its reports can find them, and reconciled monthly against their registers.<br>• <b>Fixed Asset, Depreciation, Accumulated Depreciation</b> — the asset machinery's accounts (module 7 covers fixed assets).<br>• <b>Round-off, exchange gain/loss, temporary opening</b> — the system's designated homes for its own arithmetic edges. Configured once, reviewed for reasonableness — a steadily growing round-off account is a symptom, not rounding.</p><p><b>Three type rules.</b></p><p>1. <b>Type at creation.</b> Set the type correctly when the account is born. Retyping an account that has history is an event bordering on a migration — a receivable that spent a year untyped has a year of party-less entries that no toggle repairs.<br>2. <b>When a posting misbehaves, suspect the type first.</b> The invoice that will not accept a party, the bank missing from the reconciliation list — check the account's type before names, before permissions.<br>3. <b>Defaults complete the wiring.</b> The company's default accounts — debtors, creditors, stock accounts, round-off, exchange difference — must point at correctly typed leaves. A wrong default mis-posts every document that relies on it, silently, from day one. Verify the defaults during setup, and re-verify after any chart restructuring.</p><p>Practical rule: once a year, audit the types — every Bank-type account against the real bank list, every control account against its party machinery, every default against its intended leaf. Thirty minutes, and the class of errors in rule 2 stops occurring.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The posting map", "14", "<p>This chapter makes the books-write-themselves idea concrete: the <b>posting map</b> — exactly which GL entries each document writes. Learn it once and every GL question becomes a lookup. This is the reference card of the whole track.</p><p><b>Sales Invoice.</b> DR Debtors (party: the customer) for the gross; CR Sales for the net; CR VAT Payable for the tax. Where <b>Update Stock</b> is on (the POS-style flow), the invoice also does the delivery's work: CR Stock, DR COGS.</p><p><b>Delivery Note.</b> The stock movement's money shadow: CR Stock (value leaves at the valuation method's figure), DR COGS. <b>No revenue posts here</b> — delivery moves value, invoicing books revenue — which is why a period's deliveries and invoices must both be complete before its margin is true (module 7's cut-off discipline starts here).</p><p><b>Payment Entry (received).</b> DR Bank (the payment mode's account), CR Debtors (party) — the claim becomes cash. Deductions ride as extra lines in the same entry: WHT withheld at source debits its receivable asset account alongside the bank line.</p><p><b>Purchase Receipt.</b> DR Stock at landed valuation; CR <b>Stock Received But Not Billed</b> — the accrual that says <i>we hold goods we have not yet been invoiced for</i>. Useful diagnostic: the RNB account's balance <b>is</b> the open goods-received-awaiting-invoice queue, in naira. An old, large RNB balance means unbilled receipts are aging — readable straight from the GL.</p><p><b>Purchase Invoice.</b> CR Creditors (party: the supplier); DR the RNB accrual (clearing it — the possession is now billed); DR Input VAT (the claimable asset). Where no receipt preceded it — a service invoice — the expense account is debited directly. WHT withheld from the supplier posts its credit to WHT Payable inside the payment entry that settles the invoice.</p><p><b>Stock Entries.</b> Issues and write-offs: CR Stock, DR the expense account the entry names. Material receipts: DR Stock, CR the designated offset account. Transfers: silent in the P&amp;L — value travels, nothing is earned or consumed.</p><p><b>Stock Reconciliation.</b> The count adjustment's money face: DR/CR Stock against the adjustment account — the shrinkage figure landing in the P&amp;L where policy directed it.</p><p><b>Journal Entry.</b> Whatever its author says — which is exactly its power and its danger, and why module 2 exists.</p><p><b>Using the map.</b> Three habits:</p><p>1. <b>Verify it live at setup.</b> Post one small real document of each kind and read its entries against the map. A mis-wired default or template is caught in an hour instead of inherited by a month of postings.<br>2. <b>Diagnose backwards.</b> A strange GL entry names its document; the map tells you what that document should have written; the difference locates the problem.<br>3. <b>Explain forwards.</b> When anyone asks why an account moved, the map turns the answer into one sentence — the account is written by these documents, and here are the period's instances.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Reading the GL and the trial balance", "12", "<p>The General Ledger report is the diagnostic read of this track: it answers how any balance got to be what it is. This chapter is the reading method.</p><p><b>The GL report's filters.</b></p><p>• <b>By account</b> — the account's full story between dates: opening balance, every entry, closing balance.<br>• <b>By party</b> — a customer or supplier across accounts: their whole financial relationship on one screen.<br>• <b>By voucher</b> — one document's complete footprint: the posting map, verified live.<br>• <b>By cost center or dimension</b> — module 6's lens.</p><p>Each entry shows its date, accounts, amounts, party, remarks — and its <b>document</b>, which is the point of the whole design.</p><p><b>The drill-down.</b> Every number in the system can be walked to its paper: statement line → account balance → GL entries → the document → its attachments. Three or four clicks from any report figure to the document that caused it. Use it as the standing habit:</p><p>• The disputed figure is opened live in the meeting, not researched afterwards.<br>• The strange balance is walked to its entries the same day.<br>• The month-end query is answered with a filtered GL, not a recollection.</p><p>A business whose managers drill down argues from documents; one whose managers export to spreadsheets argues from tabs. Build the first habit — it sets in the first month or not at all.</p><p><b>The trial balance.</b> The GL's summary face: every account, its period debits, credits, and closing balance. It always balances — the grammar guarantees that — so it is read not for its total but for its <b>anomalies</b>:</p><p>• <b>The account that should be zero and is not.</b> The RNB accrual aging, a suspense account with a growing balance, a round-off account trending upward.<br>• <b>The balance on the wrong side.</b> A credit-balance customer (an unallocated advance), a debit-balance VAT Payable — each one a story the grammar makes readable.<br>• <b>The account nobody recognises.</b> Chapter 9's pattern; the annual chart walk retires it.</p><p><b>The monthly scan.</b> Ten minutes, once a month: run the trial balance, flag the anomalies, open each one to its entries. This scan is the accounts layer's version of cycle counting — small and rhythmic — and it is the reason the year-end close holds no surprises. Module 7 gives the close its full checklist; the scan is the everyday version.</p><p>Practical rule: never leave a wrong-side balance or an unexplained account for the close to find. The entry that explains it is four clicks away today and an archaeology project in six months.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Control accounts and the party tie", "12", "<p>One structure deserves its own chapter before module 4 builds on it: the <b>control account</b> and its party ledger, and the tie that keeps both trustworthy.</p><p><b>The structure.</b> Debtors is <b>one leaf</b> in the chart — one balance, the total of every customer's claim. But every entry to it carried a <b>party</b> (Chapter 4's type rule), so the system maintains, in effect, a ledger per customer inside the account:</p><p>• The GL filtered by party is the customer's statement.<br>• The receivables report ages the parties.<br>• The control account's balance is, by construction, the sum of its parties' balances.</p><p>The same structure serves Creditors. The pattern: <b>the control is the total; the parties are the detail; they agree because they are the same entries read two ways.</b></p><p><b>How the tie breaks.</b> The agreement holds automatically — until someone posts around the machinery:</p><p>• A journal entry to Debtors <b>without a party</b> — a naira in the control that belongs to no customer. The total now exceeds the details, and every customer statement is silently suspect.<br>• A balance corrected at the control level while the party detail stands — the same divergence, reversed.<br>• A migration that loaded totals without party detail — the tie broken from birth.</p><p>Once diverged, reconciling a control back to its parties is archaeology. The discipline is therefore prevention:</p><p>1. <b>Party-typed accounts take party-carrying entries only.</b> The system enforces most of this; module 2's journal rules fence the rest.<br>2. <b>The monthly tie check:</b> the control account's balance against the party report's total, agreeing to the kobo. It joins the close checklist alongside the other standing agreements — stock ledger to stock accounts, tax registers to tax accounts. The shared principle: <b>a total the details cannot reproduce is a total nobody should trust.</b></p><p><b>What an intact tie buys.</b></p><p>• The ageing reports are believed — collections and the payment run both work from them.<br>• Customer and supplier statements go out without fear: the statement <b>is</b> the party ledger, printed.<br>• The auditor samples parties and finds the control every time — the confirmation exercise ends early.<br>• The balance sheet's receivables line is a number with hundreds of defensible components, not one asserted total.</p><p>Practical rule: the subledger habits taught at the sales and purchasing desks — allocate payments to invoices, match advances, carry references — are all, from this chair, tie maintenance. When the tie check disagrees, the first suspect is always a journal (module 2, Chapter 6 explains why), and the GL's journal filter on the control accounts finds it in minutes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's chart is born", "13", "<p><b>HealthTrade</b> is the pharmaceutical distributor this track follows — three branches, an in-house accountant, and a newly implemented ZhiftERP system. This chapter is the afternoon its chart was designed, following Chapters 1-7 in order.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the reading list (Chapter 3).</b> Before any account is touched, the MD's monthly questions are written down: sales and margin (eventually by branch — noted for module 6's dimensions), what running the business costs (rent, people, power, logistics — each read separately), and what the business owns and owes (banks by account, stock, debtors, creditors, the tax custodies). The accountant adds the statement's needs and the audit's. The list — fifteen questions — becomes the chart's specification.</p><p><b>Step 2 — tailoring the skeleton (Chapters 3-4).</b> The standard chart is walked against the list:</p><p>• The three real banks become three Bank-type leaves under Bank Accounts — three reconciliations coming in module 3.<br>• Debtors and Creditors are confirmed as typed control accounts.<br>• The Stock family is confirmed wired to the perpetual-inventory machinery.<br>• The tax custodies are named plainly: VAT Payable, Input VAT, WHT Payable, WHT Receivable.<br>• The expense groups are shaped to the reading list — eleven micro-splits of administrative expenses collapsed to four lines someone will actually read.<br>• The branch question is answered the right way: <b>not</b> per-branch copies of every expense account, but a dimension, noted for module 6. One chart, one language; the branches become a lens.</p><p>Names in the accountant's vocabulary; numbering per their tradition; two levels below root, nowhere three.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the posting map verified live (Chapter 5).</b> One small, real document of each kind is posted and its entries read against the map: a test invoice (Debtors gross, Sales net, VAT Payable — the grammar read aloud); a purchase receipt (the RNB accrual explained: <i>this balance is your unbilled possession — watch it clear when the invoice books</i>); a delivery's COGS line tied to the stock valuation; a payment's allocation read in the party ledger. Two defaults are corrected on the spot — the round-off account pointed at a sensible home, and one payment mode mapped to the wrong bank. The hour does exactly what Chapter 5 promised: the mis-wires are caught before a month inherits them.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the first reads (Chapters 6-7).</b> The GL drill is demonstrated to the accountant: statement → account → entries → document, four clicks. The trial balance is scanned and one anomaly found immediately — a small credit balance on a customer, drilled to an unallocated advance from the go-live week, allocated the same day. The monthly rhythms are diarised: the ten-minute trial balance scan, and the control-to-party tie check. The accountant's note at the end of the afternoon: <i>the chart answers my questions instead of the software's. That is the first chart I have ever said that about.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the first law", "11", "<p>The recurring chart-and-ledger mistakes, each with its symptom, cause and fix. Check yourself against these — they are also quiz material.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — chart sprawl.</b> Symptom: four hundred accounts for an eleven-person business; nobody can say what half are for; every report is a scroll. Cause: accounts created per whim — one per project, per branch, per mood (Chapter 3). Fix: apply the reading test in a consolidation pass — balances merged by documented entries into accounts that are actually read, unused accounts retired; creation rights narrowed; dimensions carrying the distinctions that were never account-shaped.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the suspense squatter.</b> Symptom: a suspense or miscellaneous account with a large, aging, growing balance. Cause: the account meant for momentary parking used as a destination for everything unclassifiable (Chapters 3, 6). Fix: suspense swept to zero monthly as a close-checklist line — each item classified to its true home — and the classification gaps that fed it fixed at the source: a default, a template, or training.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the untyped leaf.</b> Symptom: a bank missing from the reconciliation; an invoice refusing a party; receivables that cannot age. Cause: accounts created with names instead of types (Chapter 4) — the machinery is wired to types, and the leaf is invisible to it. Fix: type at creation as a rule; retyping-with-history treated as the serious event it is; the annual type audit.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the party-less posting.</b> Symptom: the control disagreeing with its parties; customer statements missing money everyone knows exists. Cause: entries to control accounts without parties — journals posted around the machinery (Chapter 7; module 2 owns the fence). Fix: the divergence reconciled once (priced honestly as the archaeology it is), the journal rules installed, and the monthly tie check standing guard afterwards.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the account nobody can explain.</b> Symptom: the trial balance line that draws blank looks in the meeting. Cause: the chart accumulated instead of designed (Chapter 3). Fix: the annual walk with the accountant — each unexplainable account investigated to its entries (the GL always knows), then merged, retired, or documented. The deliverable: a chart where every line has a one-sentence explanation.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can state: the direction table and DEAD; the three worked documents from Chapter 2; the reading test and the account-versus-dimension rule; the type wiring and the three type rules; the posting map document by document; the GL filters, the drill-down, and the trial balance anomaly read; the control-to-party structure, how the tie breaks, and the monthly tie check.</p><p><b>The first law: the books are written by documents, into a chart designed for reading.</b> Module 2 opens the one deliberate exception — the Journal Entry — and the rules that keep it safe.</p>"),
]

