# ============================ ACCOUNTS 2 ============================
LESSONS_A2 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The one door: the Journal Entry", "11", "<p>Module 1 described a ledger written by documents. This module covers the one deliberate exception: the <b>Journal Entry</b> — the document whose only job is to write the GL directly, any accounts, any amounts, whatever its author enters. It exists because real accounting genuinely needs it (Chapter 3 lists the lawful uses, and they are monthly). It is dangerous because of what it bypasses. This module teaches both halves.</p><p><b>What the journal bypasses.</b> Every trading document carries machinery around its GL posting:</p><p>• The invoice maintains the party ledger, feeds the VAT register, and ages into collections.<br>• The receipt writes the stock ledger beside the money.<br>• The payment allocates against its references.</p><p>The journal carries <b>none</b> of that. It writes bare GL lines, and every naira it posts has skipped the systems that make totals decomposable: the party tie, the stock-to-accounts agreement, the tax registers, the invoice match queue. That is not a flaw — the journal is <b>specialised</b> for events that have no trading document (rent accruing, depreciation charging). But the specialisation cuts both ways: used where a document exists, the journal fixes the GL's number while breaking every detail beneath it — and the break is silent. The trial balance still balances, the statements still print, and the divergence surfaces weeks later as a tie that will not reconcile. Most books rot exactly this way — not through fraud, through convenience.</p><p><b>The module's rule, stated now.</b> <b>The journal is the exception and the document is the rule: what a document can do, a document must do.</b> Sales are invoiced, never journalised. Purchases are billed. Stock moves by stock documents. Payments are payment entries. The journal takes what remains — the genuine list in Chapter 3, under the controls in Chapter 7.</p><p><b>The habit you will meet.</b> Accountants trained on manual books often journalise everything — some systems are, in practice, a GL with ten thousand journals and no trading documents at all. The habit is not incompetence; it is fluency in the old instrument. The fix is transferring the fluency: every recurring journal examined for the document that should replace it, corrections routed through Chapter 5's rules, and the monthly journal <b>count</b> watched as a health figure — a trading business's lawful journal traffic is small, so a large or growing count is itself a finding.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the journal's fields and the rules attached to each. Chapter 3: the lawful list. Chapter 4: accruals and prepayments, worked with figures. Chapter 5: the correction rules. Chapter 6: the forbidden list — journals that break machinery. Chapter 7: who may journal, and the monthly review. Chapter 8: HealthTrade's manual layer. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The journal entry, field by field", "12", "<p>The controls in this module are enforced in the journal's own fields. This chapter covers each field and its rule.</p><p><b>The rows.</b> Each row carries an account, a debit <b>or</b> credit amount, and the extras the account's type demands:</p><p>• <b>Party</b> — required where the account is a Receivable or Payable control. A journal can lawfully touch Debtors, but only carrying the customer it concerns (and Chapter 6 narrows even that).<br>• <b>Cost center / dimension</b> — where P&amp;L accounts carry their address (module 6). A journal that charges an expense must still say whose expense.<br>• <b>References</b> — where a row settles against existing entries.</p><p><b>The balance rule.</b> Total debits must equal total credits or the entry will not save. A journal that seems to need to be one-sided is a misdiagnosis: the other side exists, and the author has not found it yet. Parking the difference in suspense is not the answer — Chapter 9 covers where that habit leads.</p><p><b>Posting date.</b> When the entry speaks. Rules:</p><p>• <b>Honest dates always</b> — the accrual for March is dated in March.<br>• <b>Closed periods are respected</b> — the period-close settings block postings into finalised months (module 8 covers the settings).<br>• <b>Accrual reversals date forward</b> — the estimate posted at month-end reverses on the 1st of the next month (Chapter 4's pattern).</p><p><b>Entry type.</b> The classification field: Journal Entry, Bank Entry, Cash Entry, Contra Entry (bank-to-bank and cash-to-bank movements — module 3 uses these), Credit Note and Debit Note journals, Depreciation Entry, and others. The type does two jobs: some light machinery work (Contra entries join the bank reconciliation's world), and heavy <b>review</b> work — the monthly journal review reads by type, and a mis-typed entry hides from its reviewer. Type entries correctly.</p><p><b>Reference number and date.</b> The external anchor: the bank advice, the payroll summary, the agreement clause — the paper this journal answers to. Fill it whenever such paper exists.</p><p><b>Remarks.</b> The story field, and the most strictly enforced rule in this module: <b>every journal narrates itself</b> — what happened, why this entry, what evidence supports it. Trading documents are their own explanation; a journal's explanation lives only here. A journal with empty remarks is unusable six months later — nobody, including its author, can say why it exists, and any review (internal or audit) treats it accordingly. The remarks rule has no exceptions. Recurring templates (Chapter 7) carry their stories pre-written, so the rule costs nothing where the traffic is routine.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Rows balanced, parties on control accounts, dimensions on P&amp;L lines, honest dates, correct types, external references attached, and a story in the remarks — every time.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The lawful list", "12", "<p>The discipline of Chapter 1 only holds if the lawful uses are named, rather than negotiated case by case. This is the list — a trading business's legitimate journal traffic:</p><p><b>1. Accruals and prepayments.</b> The matching principle's instruments (Chapter 4 works them fully): expenses spread to the periods that consumed them, income deferred to the periods that earned it. The largest lawful category, and monthly by nature.</p><p><b>2. Payroll.</b> The salary journal from the payroll summary: DR salary expense (by department or dimension), CR net-pay liability (cleared by the payment run), CR the statutory custodies — PAYE, pension, other deductions — each a liability account holding money withheld for someone else. Where the payroll module automates this, the journal is machine-written; read it against the posting map once anyway. Module 5 covers the Nigerian statutory picture in full.</p><p><b>3. Depreciation.</b> DR depreciation expense, CR accumulated depreciation — machine-written where the fixed-asset machinery runs (module 7), a governed monthly journal where it does not yet.</p><p><b>4. Bank charges, interest, and bank-side events.</b> The charges the bank statement reveals: DR bank charges, CR bank — entered as Bank Entries with the statement as the reference, and only ever <b>from the reconciliation</b> (module 3), never from memory.</p><p><b>5. Opening balances.</b> The go-live event: governed, evidenced, accountant-approved, and loaded once. The critical rule: opening debtors and creditors load as <b>per-party, per-invoice detail — never one total posted to a control account</b>. The tie must be born intact. Module 4 details the migration rules.</p><p><b>6. Owner and inter-entity movements.</b> Capital introduced, drawings, director loans, movements between related entities — real events with no trading document. Journalised with the evidence referenced, with parties where applicable, and with the tax and legal shape decided by the accountant — this category most needs professional policy behind it.</p><p><b>7. Corrections.</b> Lawful only under Chapter 5's rules.</p><p><b>The two tests.</b> Whether a proposed journal belongs on this list comes down to:</p><p>1. <b>The document test.</b> Does a trading document exist for this event? If yes, the journal is forbidden regardless of convenience — the sale, the purchase, the stock move and the payment all have documents.<br>2. <b>The evidence test.</b> Can the journal name its paper — the agreement, the statement, the summary, the schedule? A journal that cannot reference evidence is an assertion, not an entry.</p><p>Practical rule: the lawful list for a given business fits on one page, and most of it recurs monthly. Chapter 7 turns that page into templates — after which anything outside the templates is, by construction, visible and reviewable.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Accruals and prepayments, worked", "13", "<p>The lawful list's largest resident gets its own chapter, because matching is the accounting idea most worth holding fluently, and its journals are the recurring set every implementation builds.</p><p><b>The principle.</b> The P&amp;L's job is to state what a period <b>earned</b> and what it <b>consumed</b> — not what it happened to pay. Cash timing and economic timing diverge constantly: rent paid yearly is consumed monthly; power consumed now is billed next month. Matching books each on its own date: the payment's date is a bank fact; the expense's date is a consumption fact. Without matching, margins lurch with payment calendars — the January that carries a whole year's rent, the fat months where no bills happened to arrive — and every monthly report reads noise.</p><p><b>Prepayment, worked.</b> HealthTrade pays annual rent of ₦2,400,000 in January.</p><p>1. <b>The payment:</b> DR Prepaid Rent ₦2,400,000, CR Bank. Prepaid Rent is an <b>asset</b> — eleven months of future occupancy is a thing the business owns.<br>2. <b>The monthly journal, twelve times:</b> DR Rent Expense ₦200,000, CR Prepaid Rent ₦200,000 — each month consuming one-twelfth of the asset into expense.</p><p>Result: January's P&amp;L carries ₦200,000 of rent, not ₦2.4m; December carries its ₦200,000 from cash paid a year earlier; and the Prepaid Rent balance at any month <b>is</b> the occupancy still owed — a balance-sheet line that means something. The recurring template (Chapter 7) writes this without anyone remembering, and the same shape serves insurance, licences and subscriptions — anything paid ahead of its consumption.</p><p><b>Accrual, worked.</b> The mirror. March consumes ₦180,000 of diesel and power, but the bills arrive in April.</p><p>1. <b>March's close journal:</b> DR Utilities Expense ₦180,000, CR Accrued Expenses (a liability — the business owes for what it consumed). March's P&amp;L now tells March's truth.<br>2. <b>The reversal:</b> the accrual reverses on April 1st — use the system's auto-reversal for exactly this.<br>3. <b>April's actual bills</b> post through their proper purchase documents. The reversal has already cleared the estimate, so the actuals land clean — no double-count.</p><p>The <b>estimate → reverse → actual</b> rhythm is the monthly craft. The close-checklist question that drives it: <i>which consumptions have no bills yet?</i></p><p><b>The income side.</b> The mirror mirrored: cash received <b>ahead</b> of earning is not income — it is a liability (unearned income) until the earning happens, then a journal moves it to income as it is earned. Deposits and retainers follow this shape.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Prepayment: pay into an asset, consume monthly into expense. Accrual: estimate the consumed-but-unbilled at close, auto-reverse on the 1st, let the actual documents land clean. Income received early: liability first, income as earned. Build all of it as templates; run it monthly, never annually.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The correction rules", "13", "<p>Mistakes happen — the wrong account, the wrong amount, the wrong month. What separates clean books from archaeology is how corrections are made. Four rules, no exceptions.</p><p><b>Rule 1 — history is never edited.</b> A posted entry is a permanent record. Corrections are <b>new entries</b> that reverse and restate, standing beside the mistake forever, telling the full story: error, discovery, repair. Even where permissions technically allow amending a posted entry, do not — an edited past is a ledger that lies about its own history, and everyone who reconciles, audits or relies on the books depends on that never happening.</p><p><b>Rule 2 — reverse and repost.</b> The mechanical form:</p><p>1. Post a <b>reversing entry</b> — the original's exact mirror: same accounts, same amounts, directions flipped. The system's reverse function builds it.<br>2. Post a <b>fresh entry</b> with the correct accounts and amounts.<br>3. Both carry references to the original and to each other, and remarks that narrate: <i>reversal of JV-0231, expense posted to wrong account; see JV-0233 for correct posting.</i></p><p>The GL then reads mistake — mirror — truth: three entries, one honest story, reconstructable by any reviewer in a minute.</p><p><b>Rule 3 — documents are corrected as documents.</b> The most-violated rule, and the one protecting everything module 1 built. When the error lives in a <b>trading document</b> — the invoice with the wrong customer, the receipt with the wrong quantity, the payment against the wrong reference — the correction happens at the <b>document</b>: cancel-and-amend where the chain allows, or the returns and credit/debit note machinery where trade has moved on. <b>Never</b> as a journal patching the GL around it.</p><p>Why: the journal patch fixes the balance while the document still tells the old story. The party ledger, the stock ledger, the tax register and the match queue now all disagree with a GL that looks right, and the standing agreements begin to diverge. The diagnostic that follows from this: <b>a tie that will not reconcile almost always has a journal in it</b> — and the fastest audit in accounting is filtering journals against the control, stock, tax and bank accounts, where honest journal traffic is nearly zero.</p><p><b>Rule 4 — the period decides the date.</b> An error found in an open month is corrected in that month, honest-dated. An error found after the close is corrected in the <b>current</b> period, with remarks naming the original period and entry — closed months are not reopened for corrections. (Module 7 covers the close; module 8 covers the settings that enforce the freeze.)</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Never edit; reverse and repost with cross-references and a story; correct documents at the document, never by journal; and date corrections by the period rule. Books kept this way remember both the mistake and the repair — which is exactly what makes them believable.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The forbidden list", "13", "<p>Some journals are not merely governed — they are forbidden, because each one breaks machinery that cannot report its own breakage. Learn the list with each entry's <i>what breaks</i>.</p><p><b>1. Revenue journals replacing invoices.</b> DR Debtors, CR Sales, no invoice. Breaks: the VAT register (output tax unbooked or untracked — a compliance failure, not a style choice), the customer's document trail (a balance with no invoice to allocate, dispute or statement), collections (nothing ages properly), and e-invoicing obligations where they apply. The sale has a document; use it. The purchase-side twin is equally forbidden: expense journals replacing supplier invoices break input VAT, WHT capture and the creditor's ledger at once.</p><p><b>2. Journals to Stock accounts.</b> Breaks: the stock-to-accounts agreement — the GL moves, the stock ledger does not, and the two diverge by exactly the journal's amount, permanently, until someone excavates. If stock value is wrong, the error is in a stock <b>document</b> — a receipt's rate, a missing entry, a reconciliation — find it and fix it there. The fence around Stock-type accounts exists for this paragraph.</p><p><b>3. Journals to control accounts.</b> Without a party: forbidden outright — a naira belonging to no customer, the tie broken (module 1, Chapter 7). Even <b>with</b> a party, treat control-account journals with suspicion: the events they usually claim — write-offs, discounts, settlement adjustments — have proper instruments (credit and debit notes, payment deductions, bad-debt provisioning in module 4) that carry the tax and ledger consequences the bare journal skips. Working rule: control-account journals are rare, party-carrying, and second-approved — or they are the disease.</p><p><b>4. Journals to tax accounts outside the filing routine.</b> Breaks: the register agreement — VAT Payable that no longer equals the invoice register's story; WHT accounts that no longer tie to certificates. Tax accounts move by documents and by the filing-and-remittance entries (module 5). The adjusting journal that forces the account to match a figure computed outside the system is backwards: reconcile the register, find the divergence, fix its document.</p><p><b>5. Journals to bank accounts outside real bank events.</b> Breaks: the bank reconciliation — the GL bank balance acquires a line the statement will never show, and the reconciliation carries an unexplained item forever. Bank accounts move by payment entries, contra entries, and the reconciliation's own charge entries — nothing else.</p><p><b>The shared shape.</b> Every item on this list is the same mistake: <b>a total adjusted around its details.</b> The GL looks right; the machinery beneath it now disagrees; and the disagreement surfaces later, further from its cause, more expensive to trace. When a standing agreement will not reconcile, filter the journals on that account family first — it is the fastest diagnosis in this track.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Journal controls: the narrow door", "12", "<p>Direct GL access is a dangerous power, and it gets the same treatment every dangerous power gets: named holders, thresholds, templates, and a monthly review.</p><p><b>Who may journal.</b> Narrow: <b>the accountant and one named backstop</b> — two people, not two departments. Never warehouse or sales operators; never branch managers — their corrections route through documents and requests, not direct GL access. A journal can move any naira anywhere; the day everyone holds the pen, every fence in Chapter 6 becomes a suggestion.</p><p><b>Thresholds and second approval.</b></p><p>• <b>Value thresholds</b> route large journals to a second sign-off, even between the two named holders — the submission workflow carries it.<br>• <b>Certain categories are second-approved regardless of value:</b> anything touching control accounts (Chapter 6's rare-and-party-carrying rule, enforced as workflow), opening and closing entries, and corrections above the materiality line.<br>• The threshold numbers are the business's own decision, written in the policy notes like every other control number.</p><p><b>Templates for the recurring set.</b> The lawful monthly traffic — accruals, prepayment consumptions, payroll, depreciation — is built <b>once</b> as templates: accounts pre-wired, dimensions pre-set, stories pre-written, amounts updated per period (or generated by the machinery where it runs). Two payoffs:</p><p>1. The template set is the manual layer's honest census — a trading business's recurring journals fit on one page.<br>2. Everything <b>outside</b> the templates is, by construction, an event: visible, reviewable, and asked about. The review question changes from <i>are these thousand journals right?</i> to <i>the recurring set ran; what are these six others?</i></p><p><b>The monthly journal review.</b> A standing line on the close checklist: every journal of the month, listed, read against five questions — <b>author, accounts, direction, date, story</b>. Ten to twenty minutes at a healthy business, because the count is small. The review reads in both directions:</p><p>• <b>Individually:</b> the forbidden account touched, the empty remarks, the date that wandered into the wrong period.<br>• <b>In aggregate:</b> the journal count trending up (the machine being declined); the same correction repeating (an upstream problem to fix at source); one author's entries clustering at period-end (a pattern that deserves a conversation).</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Two named holders; thresholds and category-based second approval; the recurring set as one page of templates; and the monthly review — five questions per entry, both directions. Under these controls the journal is what it should be: a small, well-lit room instead of a back door.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's manual layer", "13", "<p>The month HealthTrade's manual layer was built, and the three tests that proved it. Every chapter of this module appears.</p><p><b>The recurring set built (Chapters 3-4, 7).</b> The accountant and the implementation team census the genuine journal traffic and build the templates in an afternoon:</p><p>• <b>Rent:</b> the ₦2.4m January payment to Prepaid Rent; the ₦200,000 monthly consumption template, story pre-written, twelve months scheduled.<br>• <b>Payroll:</b> from the payroll summary — salary expense by dimension, net-pay liability, PAYE and pension custodies. Module 5's deep dive noted.<br>• <b>Utilities accrual:</b> the month-end estimate, auto-reversing on the 1st. The estimate-reverse-actual rhythm is demonstrated live across March and April: March's ₦180,000 accrual, April's automatic reversal, April's actual bills posting through purchase documents, the net landing clean. The accountant, seeing the reversal pattern work for the first time, adopts it for two more accrual lines unprompted.<br>• <b>Depreciation:</b> a governed monthly journal for now; the fixed-asset automation noted for module 7.</p><p>The census closes at <b>seven recurring templates — one page</b>, as Chapter 7 promised.</p><p><b>A correction done right (Chapter 5).</b> Week three: February's generator maintenance — ₦340,000 — is discovered posted to Repairs, when policy wants it in the dedicated Generator Running Costs line the MD reads separately. The temptation to edit the posted entry is declined out loud, as teaching. Instead: the mirror reversal (referenced to the original), the fresh posting to the right account (referenced to both), remarks narrating all three. The GL reads mistake — mirror — truth, and the drill-down from the MD's generator line tells the whole story in four clicks. The accountant's comment: <i>the books now remember the mistake and the repair — that is what makes them believable.</i></p><p><b>A forbidden journal refused (Chapter 6).</b> Week four: a branch manager calls the accountant directly — a customer is disputing ₦86,000; <i>just adjust the debtor and we'll sort it later.</i> The refusal is the fence working: a journal to Debtors would move the control while no document tells the customer's ledger why — the statement would show a naked adjustment and the dispute would lose its paper trail. The routing instead: the dispute is investigated, resolves as a pricing error on one line, and a <b>credit note</b> is issued for the difference — which corrects the balance, the VAT, and the customer's statement in one instrument. The manager gets the outcome he wanted; the books get it the right way.</p><p><b>Month end (Chapter 7).</b> The first monthly journal review runs: the seven templates fired on schedule, plus four other journals — each read against the five questions, one bounced back for empty remarks and resubmitted with its story. Total review time: fourteen minutes. The journal count is noted as the layer's standing health figure.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the second law", "11", "<p>The manual layer's recurring failures. Symptom, cause, fix — and the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the journal habit.</b> Symptom: hundreds of journals monthly; sales and purchases journalised; the trading modules idle. Cause: fluency in manual books never transferred to the system (Chapter 1). Fix: apply the document test to the whole traffic; migrate the biggest categories to their documents first — sales, then purchases, then payments; teach the transfer with respect; and watch the journal count as the recovery's measure.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — suspense parking.</b> Symptom: the balancing side nobody could find, parked in suspense; the account growing monthly. Cause: the one-sided instinct — posting what is known and warehousing the rest (Chapter 2). Fix: take the balance rule seriously (the other side always exists — find it or do not post), sweep suspense to zero monthly, and fix the classification gaps at their source.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the tie-breaker journals.</b> Symptom: control accounts that stopped agreeing with their parties; a stock agreement diverged by a suspiciously round number; a tax account that matches a filing but not its register. Cause: the forbidden list violated — totals adjusted around their details (Chapter 6). Fix: filter journals on the protected accounts (the fastest audit in this track), reverse each hit and re-route it through its proper instrument, re-teach the fence, and narrow the door.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the storyless journal.</b> Symptom: entries whose remarks are empty, cryptic, or say <i>adjustment</i>; reviews that stall on them; authors who cannot recall them. Cause: the remarks rule unenforced (Chapter 2). Fix: require the story at entry, bounce storyless journals back in the monthly review, and let templates carry pre-written stories so routine traffic is never the problem.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the year-end journal storm.</b> Symptom: a quiet ledger all year, then fifty journals in the close week — accruals never run monthly, corrections hoarded, adjustments landing in one avalanche. Cause: the manual layer run annually instead of monthly (Chapters 4, 7) — every month's statements wrong all year, corrected once, when nobody remembers anything. Fix: the recurring set on its monthly rhythm, corrections posted when found, the monthly review keeping the layer current.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can state: what the journal bypasses and why the break is silent; the fields and their rules (balance, party, date, type, reference, remarks); the lawful list and the two tests; the prepayment and accrual worked examples and the estimate-reverse-actual rhythm; the four correction rules; the forbidden list with what each breaks; and the controls — two holders, thresholds, templates, the five-question monthly review.</p><p><b>The second law: the journal is the exception and the document is the rule — what a document can do, a document must do.</b> Module 3 takes the books to their outside witness: the bank.</p>"),
]

